[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.6cm, y=0.7cm]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) rectangle (0.5,2);
\fill[gray!35] (0.5,0) rectangle (1,2.25);
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (1.5,3);
\fill[gray!35] (1.5,0) rectangle (2,4.25);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (0.5,2);
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1,2.25);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (1.5,3);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2,4.25);
\draw[->] (-0.3,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.8) -- (0,8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x+2});
\node at (0.6,6.4) {$y=x^{2}+2$};
\foreach \x in {0.5,1,1.5,2} \draw (\x,0.16) -- (\x,-0.16) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The area under $y=x^{2}+2$ from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is estimated with the $4$ left-endpoint rectangles shown. What is the estimate?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.3cm, y=0.45cm]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) rectangle (1,9);
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (2,6);
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) rectangle (3,1);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (1,9);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (2,6);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (3,1);
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.4) -- (0,12.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:3.15,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{10-\x*\x});
\node at (2.9,9) {$y=10-x^{2}$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.4) -- (\x,-0.4) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10} \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The area under $y=10-x^{2}$ from $x=0$ to $x=3$ is estimated with the $3$ right-endpoint rectangles shown. What is the estimate?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.2cm]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (2,5.25);
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) rectangle (3,9.25);
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) rectangle (4,15.25);
\fill[gray!35] (4,0) rectangle (5,23.25);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (2,5.25);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (3,9.25);
\draw (3,0) rectangle (4,15.25);
\draw (4,0) rectangle (5,23.25);
\draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (6.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.4) -- (0,35) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:5.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x+3});
\fill (1.5,5.25) circle (2pt);
\fill (2.5,9.25) circle (2pt);
\fill (3.5,15.25) circle (2pt);
\fill (4.5,23.25) circle (2pt);
\node at (1.4,28) {$y=x^{2}+3$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,1) -- (\x,-1) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {10,20,30} \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The $4$ rectangles shown take their heights from the midpoint of each strip, marked by a dot. What is the midpoint estimate for the area under $y=x^{2}+3$ from $x=1$ to $x=5$?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.2cm, y=0.3cm]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) -- (1,2) -- (2,5) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) -- (2,5) -- (3,10) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) -- (3,10) -- (4,17) -- (4,0) -- cycle;
\draw (1,0) -- (1,2) -- (2,5) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw (2,0) -- (2,5) -- (3,10) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw (3,0) -- (3,10) -- (4,17) -- (4,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.4) -- (0,22) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:4.3,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x+1});
\node at (1.2,17.5) {$y=x^{2}+1$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} \draw (\x,0.6) -- (\x,-0.6) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {5,10,15,20} \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the $3$ trapezia shown to estimate the area under $y=x^{2}+1$ from $x=1$ to $x=4$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.4cm]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (2,10);
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) rectangle (3,5);
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) rectangle (4,3.333);
\fill[gray!35] (4,0) rectangle (5,2.5);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (2,10);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (3,5);
\draw (3,0) rectangle (4,3.333);
\draw (4,0) rectangle (5,2.5);
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (7.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.4) -- (0,13.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=0.85:6.5,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{10/\x});
\node at (4.4,8) {$y=\frac{10}{x}$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6} \draw (\x,0.3) -- (\x,-0.3) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10} \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The area under $y=\frac{10}{x}$ from $x=1$ to $x=5$ is estimated with the $4$ left-endpoint rectangles shown. Which statement about this estimate is correct?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.5cm, y=0.55cm]
\fill[gray!35] (0.5,0) rectangle (1,0.25);
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (1.5,1);
\fill[gray!35] (1.5,0) rectangle (2,2.25);
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) rectangle (2.5,4);
\fill[gray!35] (2.5,0) rectangle (3,6.25);
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1,0.25);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (1.5,1);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2,2.25);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (2.5,4);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3,6.25);
\draw[->] (-0.3,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,12) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:3.3,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x});
\node at (1,9) {$y=x^{2}$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.3) -- (\x,-0.3) node[below] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10} \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The area under $y=x^{2}$ from $x=0$ to $x=3$ is estimated with $6$ left-endpoint strips. The first rectangle has height $f(0)=0$, so it is not visible. What is the estimate?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.6cm, y=0.5cm]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (1.4,3.6);
\fill[gray!35] (1.4,0) rectangle (1.8,4.8);
\fill[gray!35] (1.8,0) rectangle (2.2,6);
\fill[gray!35] (2.2,0) rectangle (2.6,7.2);
\fill[gray!35] (2.6,0) rectangle (3,8.4);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (1.4,3.6);
\draw (1.4,0) rectangle (1.8,4.8);
\draw (1.8,0) rectangle (2.2,6);
\draw (2.2,0) rectangle (2.6,7.2);
\draw (2.6,0) rectangle (3,8.4);
\draw[->] (-0.3,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,10.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (0.7,1.5) -- (3.3,9.3);
\node at (1.1,9.2) {$y=f(x)$};
\draw (1,0.3) -- (1,-0.3) node[below] {$1$};
\draw (3,0.3) -- (3,-0.3) node[below] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The area under $y=f(x)$ from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is estimated with the $5$ right-endpoint rectangles shown, each of width $0.4$. The estimate comes to $12$ square units. What is the sum of the five rectangle heights?
